## Supported platforms

Prebuilt native binaries ship for:

- macOS on Apple silicon (`darwin-arm64`, package `@orin-dx/michi-darwin-arm64`)
- macOS on Intel (`darwin-x64`, package `@orin-dx/michi-darwin-x64`)
- `renderToon`, `renderHints`, `appendHints`, `renderRecovery`, `renderKv`, `renderGithubAnnotation`, and `AgentResponse.items` / `kvItems` / `error` / `partialSuccess` / `pipeline` / `status` / `alreadyDone`. An input has the same limits wherever it is accepted.
- Array lengths are checked first: at most 100,000 rows, 1,000 fields, values per row, KV items or status items, 10,000 hints, 10,000 `AgentResponse.error` causes, and 10,000 entries in each of `AgentResponse.partialSuccess`' `completed`, `failed` and `skipped` and `AgentResponse.pipeline`'s `steps`. For rows, every row's length is read and summed before any cell is converted.
- Then each value is converted against a per-call budget: at most **1,000,000 cells** and **16 MiB of string data** in all (see "Output size" below). A string's UTF-8 length is read, and charged, before it is copied, so a JS string referenced many times is counted every time it is used.
- Per-string caps: **1 MiB** for a cell value (`strVal`), hint, error cause, recovery or failed-op `reason`, status `description`/`health`, error `message`, `summary` or pipeline step `name`; **4 KiB** for `typeName`, a field name, a KV or status key, `truncationHint`, a recovery `tool`, `toolName`, an error `code`, `operation`, a completed or skipped step id, and a pipeline step's `id`, `status` and `reason`. `appendHints`' `body` counts toward the 16 MiB budget only.
- Errors keep the usual wording: `rows length 100001 exceeds maximum of 100000`, `cell count 1000100 exceeds maximum of 1000000`, `input byte total 16777226 exceeds maximum of 16777216`, `strVal byte length 1200000 exceeds maximum of 1048576`. A string whose UTF-16 length already exceeds the cap is rejected without scanning it, reported as `byte length (at least N)`.
- Only the payload a cell's `type` names is read (`strVal` for `"str"`, `floatVal` and `decimalsVal` for `"float"`, …). As before, an object field set to `null` is a wrong-typed value, not an absent one.
- The single-string `AgentResponse` members (the constructor's `typeName`, `hint`, `recoveryHint`, `truncationHint`, `humanContent`) keep plain `string` parameters, since napi copies their one argument exactly once; they reject a value over the caps after the copy: 4 KiB for `typeName`, a recovery `tool` and `truncationHint`, 1 MiB for a hint, a recovery `reason` and `humanContent`. `AgentResponse.hint`/`recoveryHint` also keep their cumulative 10,000-call caps.
- **Cumulative budget per `AgentResponse`:** one object holds at most **16 MiB** across all its calls: the caller strings (the type name, `items` fields and cells, `kvItems` keys and values, hints, recovery tools and reasons, the truncation hint and `humanContent`), the storage rows and cells keep, and the repeats the output writes (see "Output size"). `items`, `kvItems`, `hint` and `recoveryHint` each add a block, so their bytes add to the total; `truncationHint` and `humanContent` replace their slot, so the old value's bytes come back first. A call that would pass the total throws `cumulative byte total 16777217 exceeds maximum of 16777216` and leaves the builder unchanged. Before, each call had its own budget, so 65 separate 1 MiB hints were accepted and rendered 68 MB synchronously.
- **Output size:** the byte budget bounds what a render writes, not only what it reads. On every surface, a render is at most **19 times the bytes held, plus 4 KiB**. A control character is the worst case for a string: six bytes in the text (`\u0001`), seven in the JSON `body` (the text, escaped again) and six in the raw `sections`, `error` and `hints` copies, so one held byte is at most 6 + 7 + 6 = 19 bytes across the text and the JSON that `toCallToolResult()` returns together. A name is written in more places than that, so the budget charges each name for every place a writer can put it, not once: a `truncated` row writes the cell's field name, and, when two or more sections are cut, its section name, again for every cut cell (a string cell longer than 200 chars; no export changes that cap), so a field name is charged once per cut-capable cell of its column, and the type name or an outcome section's name once per cut-capable cell of its table; a section's name is charged twice (its `name[N]{…}:` header or `name: []` line and its row of the `totalCount` table; the type name's second charge is the one made when the response is built); a hint is charged one byte more than its length and a recovery hint four, for the row and the JSON object that hold them. Before, each name was charged once, and a 4 KiB field name with 1,000 rows of one 201-char cell (261 KB held) rendered to 24.8 MB of text and 29.1 MB of JSON, 95 and 112 times what it held; at 60,000 rows, under the old cap, `renderText()` failed with `Failed to convert rust String into napi string`. That table now holds about 4.4 KB per row, so 3,859 rows fit and 60,000 are rejected with `cumulative byte total N exceeds maximum of 16777216` (`input byte total N …` for `renderToon`, which charges the same names for its one list). The one-shot renders write text only: at most 6 bytes per byte charged, 26 per cell, 4 per row and 400 per other entry, which the counts cap under 200 MiB (a compile-time assertion). The tests render a matrix of hostile responses (every escape trigger and multi-byte characters; 4 KiB type, section and field names; cut cells in every composed section; numbers at their widest; each tiny item at its cap, alone) and assert that no surface passes the bound, with mutants (a factor of 18, a field or section name left uncharged) failing it; the Node suite builds the largest hostile table the budget accepts and measures all three formats against V8's string limit (about 512 MiB, `2^29 - 24` characters). At the old 64 MiB cap a maximal response (60 one-MiB control-character values) wrote 377 MB of text on a 1.2 GiB heap, and `toCallToolResult()` took 25 s and 3.2 GiB; at 16 MiB the worst case is 304 MiB, under the string limit, so a response either renders in every format or is rejected by the budget before anything is rendered. The largest accepted plain one (15 one-MiB control-character values) took 0.6 s and 348 MiB for `renderText()`, 2.8 s and 648 MiB for `renderJson()`, and 5.5 s and 838 MiB for `toCallToolResult()`, in a debug build.
- michi-core's own shape caps (1,000 fields, 1,000,000 cells, a 256-char truncation hint; see [02 — Size limits](02-toon-format.md#size-limits)) still apply after conversion and surface as `toon_validation_failed: …`.

  })
})

void describe('AgentResponse cumulative byte budget', () => {
  const mib = 'x'.repeat(1_048_576)

  void it('caps hints at 16 MiB across calls, not per call', () => {
    // 65 separate 1 MiB hints were all accepted and rendered 68 MB synchronously (at a 64 MiB cap).
    const r = new AgentResponse('t')
    let accepted = 0
    assert.throws(
      () => {
        for (let i = 0; i < 17; i++) {
          r.hint(mib)
          accepted++
        }
      },
      // Each hint is charged its bytes and one more for the quotes and separator it is written with.
      (err) => err.message === `cumulative byte total ${1 + 16 * (1_048_576 + 1)} exceeds maximum of 16777216`
    )
    assert.strictEqual(accepted, 15, '"t" takes one byte, so the 16th full hint passes 16 MiB')
    assert.ok(r.renderText().length < 16_777_216 + 4096)
  })

  void it('charges recovery hints, items and kvItems to the same total', () => {
    const r = new AgentResponse('t')
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) r.recoveryHint('retry', mib)
    const rows = Array.from({ length: 10 }, () => [{ type: 'str', strVal: mib }])
    assert.throws(() => r.items(rows, ['v']), /^Error: cumulative byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/)
    assert.ok(r.renderText().startsWith('t: []'), 'the builder is unchanged on error')
    r.items(rows.slice(0, 4), ['v'])
    // Every items call adds a section, so a second 4 MiB list adds to the first: 10 + 4 + 4 passes 16 MiB.
    assert.throws(() => r.items(rows.slice(0, 4), ['v']), /cumulative byte total/)
    const kv = Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, i) => ({ key: `k${i}`, value: { type: 'str', strVal: mib } }))
    assert.throws(() => r.kvItems(kv), /cumulative byte total/, '10 + 4 + 5 MiB passes 16 MiB')
    r.kvItems(kv.slice(0, 1))
  })

  void it('caps the constructor type name and humanContent', () => {
    assert.throws(() => new AgentResponse('t'.repeat(4097)), {
      message: 'typeName byte length 4097 exceeds maximum of 4096',
  })
})

// The byte cap bounds the output as well as the input. Rendering writes a control character as `\u0001` in the text,
// again escaped in the JSON, and the JSON again beside the text in toCallToolResult, so a render is at most 19 times
// the bytes held. At 16 MiB that is 304 MiB, under V8's string limit (2^29 - 24 characters). At the old 64 MiB cap a
// maximal response produced 377 MB of text, a renderJson string V8 could not hold (GenericFailure after rendering it),
// and a toCallToolResult that took 25 s and 3 GiB.
void describe('output size at the byte cap', () => {
  const V8_MAX_STRING = 2 ** 29 - 24
  const mib = 1_048_576

  void it('renders the largest accepted control-character response in every format, or rejects it up front', () => {
    const control = '\u0001'.repeat(mib)
    const r = new AgentResponse('t')
    let accepted = 0
    assert.throws(
      () => {
        for (let i = 0; i < 17; i++) {
          r.kvItems([{ key: `k${i}`, value: { type: 'str', strVal: control } }])
          accepted++
        }
      },
      (err) => /^cumulative byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/.test(err.message)
    )
    // Each value takes 1 MiB plus its key and storage, so 15 fit and the 16th is rejected before anything renders.
    assert.strictEqual(accepted, 15)

    const text = r.renderText()
    const json = r.renderJson()
    const result = r.toCallToolResult()
    const held = 15 * mib
    assert.ok(text.length <= 6 * held + 4096, `text is ${text.length} chars`)
    assert.ok(json.length <= 13 * held + 4096, `json is ${json.length} chars`)
    assert.ok(Math.max(text.length, json.length) < V8_MAX_STRING)
    assert.strictEqual(result.content[0].text, text)
    assert.deepStrictEqual(Object.keys(result.structuredContent), ['body', 'sections', 'hints', 'recovery', 'isError'])
  })

  void it('rejects a single call over the cap with a clear error, without rendering', () => {
    const hints = Array.from({ length: 17 }, () => 'h'.repeat(mib))
    assert.throws(() => renderHints(hints), /^Error: input byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/)
  })

  // A `truncated` row writes the field name, and, beside another cut section, the section name, again for every cut
  // cell, so those names are charged for every cell longer than 200 chars. A 4 KiB control-character field name with
  // 1,000 rows of one 201-char cell used to hold 261 KB and render to 95 times that in text and 112 times in JSON; at
  // 60,000 rows the input was under the cap and `renderText()` failed to hand V8 a string it could not hold.
  const longName = '\u0001'.repeat(4096)
  const cut = (n) => Array.from({ length: n }, () => [{ type: 'str', strVal: 'a'.repeat(201) }])
  const cutSteps = (n) =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => ({ id: 'a'.repeat(201), name: 'n', status: 'completed' }))

  // The most of `n` that `accepts` takes: `accepts(n)` is true for the result and false for one more.
  const largest = (accepts, hi) => {
    let lo = 0
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2)
      if (accepts(mid)) lo = mid
      else hi = mid - 1
    }
    return lo
  }
  const accepts = (build) => (n) => {
    try {
      build(n)
      return true
    } catch (err) {
      assert.match(err.message, /^(cumulative|input) byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/)
      return false
    }
  }

  void it('rejects the 60,000-row table whose field name a truncated row would write 60,000 times', () => {
    const r = new AgentResponse('t')
    assert.throws(
      () => r.items(cut(60_000), [longName]),
      /^Error: cumulative byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/
    )
    assert.throws(
      () => renderToon({ typeName: 't', fields: [longName], rows: cut(60_000) }),
      /^Error: input byte total \d+ exceeds maximum of 16777216$/
    )
  })

  void it('renders the largest long-field-name table it accepts, in every format', () => {
    const build = (n) => {
      const r = new AgentResponse('t')
      r.items(cut(n), [longName])
      return r
    }
    const n = largest(accepts(build), 5000)
    // About 16 MiB / (4 KiB name + the cell) rows: far fewer than the 60,000 that used to be accepted.
    assert.ok(n > 3000 && n < 4500, `${n} rows accepted`)
    const r = build(n)
    const text = r.renderText()
    const json = r.renderJson()
    const result = r.toCallToolResult()
    // The name and the cell are held at least once per row, so the output stays under 19 times that.
    const held = n * (4096 + 201)
    assert.ok(text.length + json.length <= 19 * held, `${text.length + json.length} chars for ${held} held`)
    assert.ok(Math.max(text.length, json.length) < V8_MAX_STRING)
    assert.strictEqual(result.content[0].text, text)

    const toon = (rows) => renderToon({ typeName: 't', fields: [longName], rows: cut(rows) })
    const m = largest(accepts(toon), 5000)
    assert.ok(m > 3000 && m < 4500, `${m} rows accepted`)
    const out = toon(m)
    assert.ok(out.length <= 19 * m * (4096 + 201), `${out.length} chars for ${m} rows`)
    assert.ok(out.length < V8_MAX_STRING)

